# The Invisible Scale: An Interactive Journey

Begin with a normalized model whose raw weights are $e^a\log(1+b x_i)$. The exponential looks dominant, but normalization only remembers proportions. Move the scale yourself:

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The raw mass ranges across orders of magnitude while the probability bars stay fixed. This is the same invariance familiar from [softmax normalization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Softmax_function), where a common logit shift is invisible.

## See the normalized distribution

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the cancellation argument</summary>

Let $A(b)=\sum_i\log(1+b x_i)$. The partition function is $Z(a,b)=e^aA(b)$, and therefore $p_i=e^a\log(1+b x_i)/(e^aA(b))=\log(1+b x_i)/A(b)$ whenever $A(b)\ne0$. Thus every value of $a$ gives the same distribution at fixed $b$.

</details>

## What still changes?

The shape parameter changes relative weights rather than common scale. Explore its score and Fisher information:

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact shape score</summary>

For positive inputs, $S_b(i)=x_i/((1+b x_i)\log(1+b x_i))-A'(b)/A(b)$, where $A'(b)=\sum_j x_j/(1+b x_j)$. Its probability-weighted mean is zero, and its weighted second moment is the surviving shape information.

</details>

## The Fisher obstruction

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the metric proof</summary>

Because the scale score is identically zero, the Fisher matrix is $\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\0&I_{bb}\end{pmatrix}$. Its determinant is zero and the nonzero vector $(1,0)$ has zero squared Fisher length. A nondegenerate two-dimensional Riemannian metric therefore does not exist in these coordinates.

</details>

## Numerical stability and model redesign

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

Computing the reduced formula avoids overflow from unnecessary exponentiation. To recover two identifiable coordinates, place the exponential around a sample-dependent feature, remove the redundant scale, or quotient parameters by equality of their induced distributions. For background, see introductions to [Fisher information](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fisher_information) and [identifiability](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identifiability).
